name: sibling-enforcement-drift
kind: inferred
shape: categorical
face: in
membrane: false
note: |
  Fires when two codepaths that must enforce the SAME rule hold different
  versions of it — one was fixed, the sibling never got the ruling. The
  tell is asymmetric strictness on identical input: a router rejects what
  the grammar admits, or a checker admits what the grammar rejects.

  Witnessed 2026-10-05 in "bridge: the segmenter's alphabet is the wire's
  alphabet": runtime.wirescan.kz had already ruled `'` a character, not a
  delimiter; bridge.turn.kz's scanLineChars never got the ruling and a
  narration apostrophe swallowed the next wire item whole. Same shape as
  the parsewire header declaring a validation-error nobody had built —
  the divergence is invisible until a live input lands in the seam.

  Detector: enumerate the rule's enforcement surfaces (parsers, scanners,
  routers, checkers sharing one grammar) and diff their literal sets —
  delimiters, keywords, spellings — not just their pass/fail outputs.
